      "decision_index": 9,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Mishra's Bauble was used to look at the top card of their own library instead of the opponent's. Looking at the opponent's top card provides information about what they will draw next, which is generally more valuable since you already have some control over your own draws (e.g., via Preordain in hand). Knowing the opponent's next draw helps inform decisions about when to use counterspells, combat tricks, etc.",
      "action_taken": "Targeted themselves with Mishra's Bauble, looking at their own top card.",
      "better_line": "Target Sonnet Spike (the opponent) with Mishra's Bauble to gain information about what the opponent will draw next turn. Since Llama4 Valley has Preordain in hand which can already manipulate their own top cards, the Bauble is better used for scouting the opponent's draw.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 8,
      "player": "Llama4 Valley",
      "decision_index": 9,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Fetching a basic Mountain instead of Steam Vents means Llama4 Valley only has red mana available. They have Preordain (U) and Slickshot Show-Off (1R) in hand, and will need blue mana soon. With Steam Vents already in hand, fetching the basic Mountain means they'll need to play Steam Vents next turn (likely paying 2 life to have it untapped), whereas fetching Steam Vents now would give them both colors immediately and let them keep the Steam Vents in hand as a future land drop. However, this is from a Bloodstained Mire fetch (already resolved earlier), and they already have a Mountain on the battlefield. Fetching Steam Vents would give them access to blue mana this turn to cast Preordain, which is important for setting up their Slickshot Show-Off game plan. Instead, by fetching basic Mountain, they delay blue mana access by a turn.",
      "action_taken": "Fetched basic Mountain from Bloodstained Mire",
      "better_line": "Fetch Steam Vents (paying 2 life to have it enter untapped) to have blue mana available this turn for Preordain, enabling better card selection and earlier setup for the Slickshot Show-Off combo.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 19,
      "player": "Llama4 Valley",
      "decision_index": 18,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Casting Mutagenic Growth in the precombat main phase with no opponent interaction to respond to is wasteful. The opponent has only an Island untapped, so there's no combat trick needed preemptively. More importantly, casting it now means paying 2 life for just +2/+2 on a 1/2 flyer with no other spells being cast this turn to stack triggers. Slickshot Show-Off triggers on noncreature spells giving +2/+0, so casting Mutagenic Growth gives +2/+0 (trigger) + +2/+2 (the spell itself) = +4/+2, making it a 5/4 for one attack dealing 5 damage. However, the opponent could have Spell Snare (which counters MV 2 spells - doesn't apply here since Mutagenic Growth is MV 1). But the real issue is sequencing: they should play Steam Vents first (land drop), then cast Preordain to dig for more spells, and chain multiple noncreature spells together with Slickshot to maximize damage. Casting Mutagenic Growth before Preordain means fewer trigger stacks. The optimal line is: play Steam Vents, cast Preordain (Slickshot triggers +2/+0), then cast Violent Urge (another +2/+0 trigger plus +1/+0 and first strike), then Mutagenic Growth (another +2/+0 trigger plus +2/+2), resulting in a much larger attack. Spending the Mutagenic Growth first without chaining is a significant sequencing error that reduces total damage output.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Mutagenic Growth on Slickshot Show-Off in precombat main before playing land or casting other spells",
      "better_line": "Play Steam Vents, then cast Preordain to dig for more spells, then move to combat and chain Violent Urge + Mutagenic Growth(s) to maximize Slickshot Show-Off's trigger count and total damage",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 22,
      "player": "Llama4 Valley",
      "decision_index": 20,
    },
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Opponent is at 8 life with Lightning Bolt (3 damage) in hand, but the player also has Violent Urge which is only useful with creatures. Preordain would help find another burn spell or Slickshot Show-Off to close the game. However, the critical issue is that Lightning Bolt to face only puts opponent to 5, while Preordain could find additional burn to set up lethal. More importantly, the player should cast Preordain FIRST to scry and potentially find another burn spell or creature, then decide whether to bolt face or hold it. Casting Lightning Bolt now before Preordain is poor sequencing - Preordain costs U (1 mana) and Lightning Bolt costs R (1 mana), and with 3 lands available (Mountain, Mountain, Steam Vents), the player can cast both this turn. By casting Preordain first, they see what they draw and can make a more informed decision about whether to bolt face now or save it. Actually, they CAN cast both - Preordain for U off Steam Vents, then Lightning Bolt for R off a Mountain. Casting bolt first wastes the opportunity to sequence optimally, though both can be cast this turn. The bolt to face for 3 when opponent is at 8 is fine as part of a burn plan, but Preordain first is strictly better sequencing since the information from Preordain could affect targeting decisions for Lightning Bolt.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Lightning Bolt (presumably targeting opponent's face) before casting Preordain",
